1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,374
Can you show me GitHub Issues?

2
00:00:07,933 --> 00:00:09,269
Issues track work:

3
00:00:09,269 --> 00:00:11,495
bugs, feature requests, tasks.

4
00:00:11,495 --> 00:00:16,615
The useful part for Git users is the link between issues and commits:

5
00:00:16,615 --> 00:00:24,776
writing Fixes #12 in a commit or PR closes the issue automatically when the change reaches the default branch.

6
00:00:24,776 --> 00:00:28,338
We run that workflow on the practice repository.

7
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:34,499
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

8
00:00:35,033 --> 00:00:36,778
Report the bug:

9
00:00:37,333 --> 00:00:43,126
Fix it with a commit that references the issue, and push to main (on a team:

10
00:00:43,126 --> 00:00:46,633
through a PR whose description says Fixes #N):

11
00:00:47,167 --> 00:00:52,420
Fix it with a commit that references the issue, and push to main (on a team:

12
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,600
through a PR whose description says Fixes #N):

13
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:00,646
GitHub closes the issue and links the commit (it can take a few seconds):

14
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:03,985
Let me try something.

15
00:01:03,985 --> 00:01:10,855
Fix the chai issue on a feature branch, with the closing keyword, and push only the branch:

16
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:12,143
Still OPEN:

17
00:01:12,143 --> 00:01:17,345
closing keywords act only when the commit lands on the default branch (main).

18
00:01:17,345 --> 00:01:23,628
On a feature branch the commit is linked from the issue's timeline ("referenced this issue"),

19
00:01:23,628 --> 00:01:27,344
but the work is not considered done until it is merged.

20
00:01:27,344 --> 00:01:28,493
That is intended:

21
00:01:28,493 --> 00:01:30,520
the fix might never be merged.

22
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:38,181
Merge it into main (here directly; on a team, by merging the PR):

23
00:01:39,933 --> 00:01:40,647
Got it.

24
00:01:40,647 --> 00:01:43,398
What do I need to remember?

25
00:01:43,933 --> 00:01:50,643
Issues track work; gh issue create/list/view/comment/close.

26
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:58,915
Fixes #N / Closes #N in a commit or PR closes the issue when it reaches the default branch.

27
00:01:59,467 --> 00:02:04,371
On other branches the commit is only referenced; merging closes it.

28
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:08,793
I keep hearing about labels.

29
00:02:08,793 --> 00:02:10,598
What is it, really?

30
00:02:11,133 --> 00:02:14,794
Labels categorise issues and pull requests:

31
00:02:14,794 --> 00:02:21,690
type (bug, enhancement), priority, area (helm, terraform), status (needs-review).

32
00:02:21,690 --> 00:02:27,735
They make lists filterable and drive automation (a release-notes label,

33
00:02:27,735 --> 00:02:31,481
a CI job that runs only for deploy-preview).

34
00:02:31,481 --> 00:02:35,568
We create labels, apply them and filter by them.

35
00:02:37,333 --> 00:02:41,733
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

36
00:02:42,267 --> 00:02:45,044
Every new repository has default labels:

37
00:02:45,044 --> 00:02:50,181
Create the team's own labels (--force updates them if they already exist):

38
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:55,589
Create the team's own labels (--force updates them if they already exist):

39
00:02:55,589 --> 00:02:58,083
Open an issue with labels, and filter:

40
00:02:58,633 --> 00:03:01,923
Open an issue with labels, and filter:

41
00:03:03,667 --> 00:03:05,668
Let me try something.

42
00:03:05,668 --> 00:03:08,051
A typo in the label name:

43
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:10,518
'priority:hihg' not found:

44
00:03:10,518 --> 00:03:21,365
labels must exist in the repository before they can be applied (the web interface only offers existing ones; the CLI and API refuse unknown names).

45
00:03:21,365 --> 00:03:23,210
Check the exact spelling:

46
00:03:24,967 --> 00:03:26,766
Here is the fix.

47
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:29,213
Got it.

48
00:03:29,213 --> 00:03:31,965
What do I need to remember?

49
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:39,145
Labels categorise issues and PRs by type, priority, area, status.

50
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:46,725
gh label create/list/edit/delete; apply with gh issue edit --add-label.

51
00:03:47,267 --> 00:03:52,621
Labels must exist before use; share one scheme across repositories.

52
00:03:54,367 --> 00:03:56,781
How does milestones work?

53
00:03:57,333 --> 00:04:03,989
A milestone groups issues and pull requests toward a goal with an optional due date,

54
00:04:03,989 --> 00:04:06,445
typically a release ("v1.1.0").

55
00:04:06,445 --> 00:04:10,802
GitHub shows its progress as the share of closed items.

56
00:04:10,802 --> 00:04:15,873
We create one, assign issues, close them, and read the progress.

57
00:04:17,633 --> 00:04:22,033
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

58
00:04:22,567 --> 00:04:27,467
The CLI has no milestone command; the API does it (on the web:

59
00:04:27,467 --> 00:04:30,391
Issues → Milestones → New milestone):

60
00:04:30,391 --> 00:04:32,446
Plan three issues into it:

61
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,479
Plan three issues into it:

62
00:04:36,033 --> 00:04:37,588
Two are done:

63
00:04:39,333 --> 00:04:40,899
Let me try something.

64
